SYNOPSIS
        mapping update_blueprint_result(int id)

DESCRIPTION
        Read a fresh, non-consuming report for update_blueprint(). Only the
        requesting object or the master may read it. Unknown, cancelled,
        expired, evicted, and unauthorized identifiers raise an error.
        Polling does not execute or advance a request.

        The mapping always has these thirteen fields:

          id                    Positive, driver-lifetime request identifier.
          origin                Canonical load name, with leading /, without .c.
          selection             "all" or "explicit".
          status                "pending", "completed", or "failed".
          candidate_generation  Exact candidate's numeric generation label.
          matched               Surviving selected clones, including current ones.
          updated               Selected clones that received the candidate.
          already_current       Clones already using the exact candidate program.
          destroyed             Destroyed identities in the saved explicit set.
          blueprint_updated     1 if the path-source blueprint was updated, else 0.
          variable_changes      Array of generation-specific schema evidence.
          errors                Array of reasons and diagnostic records.
          completed_at          Driver completion time in seconds.

        While pending, generation/count/time fields are zero and both arrays
        are empty, including during compiler callbacks. These zeros mean
        unknown, not an empty terminal selection. Clone counts exclude the
        blueprint. All mode has no admission snapshot from which to count
        earlier destruction. Survivor failures retain complete discovered
        counts; if discovery itself exceeds its scan bound, counts remain
        zero and the reason says that selection counts are unavailable.
        Generation labels identify exact programs; they are not source hashes,
        persistent versions, or object creation times.

        Each variable_changes row has old_generation, candidate_generation,
        matched, added, removed, functions, and blockers. Here matched is an
        array of retained variable declarations, not a clone count. Added and
        removed are variable declaration arrays; functions describes old/new
        function locations, including removals. A function may be removed if
        no retained supported handle needs it. Each actual old generation
        appears once, even when many selected clones share it.

        Declaration records have program (canonical declaring load name),
        name, occurrence (inheritance occurrence path), old_slot, new_slot,
        and generated (0 or 1). A missing side has slot -1. Physical slots
        may change when variables/functions are added, removed, or reordered;
        slots are locations, not declaration identity. Added variables also
        have a default mapping. Function signature/argument evidence is
        captured independently of pragma save_types; save_types is not a
        blanket prerequisite for updates.

        Default mappings are conditional:

          kind "shared"       Copy the blueprint's prepared/current value;
                              no live value is exposed in the report.
          kind "missing"      Required metadata is absent; may contain only kind.
          kind "unavailable"  Evidence or materialization is unavailable/invalid.
          kind "unsupported"  Captured initializer is unsupported.
          kind "literal"      Supported literal, with native value.
          kind "implicit"     Implicit integer or float zero, with value.

        File and line identify the initializer when available. Unsupported
        or unavailable capture may include numeric reason: 0 none, 1 syntax,
        2 capture/storage limit, 3 unrepresentable number, 4 invalid mapping
        width, 5 missing/invalid metadata (reserved; not currently emitted
        by compiler capture). An unavailable materialization may contain
        value 0; that does not make the default usable. A successful literal
        or implicit value can have type_warning: 1 under warn_rtt_checks.
        Retained initializers are not replayed. Literal report values contain
        only native scalar/array/mapping data, never live object/handle values.

        Path-source mode adds an implicit blueprint generation row even
        without clones. It also has blueprint: 1, blueprint_defaults (added
        declarations with the blueprint's default decisions), and
        blueprint_blockers. Its ordinary added decisions describe clone
        defaults if that generation has selected clones, otherwise blueprint
        defaults. Thus a literal blueprint default and a clone's shared
        decision remain distinguishable for the same generation. Object-source
        mode does not implicitly migrate its loaded blueprint.

        Failed requests begin errors with code and message for the primary
        failure. Structural/source checks precede schema, native/Python runtime
        dependencies, and named declaration resolution. Existing compiler and
        schema-limit reasons keep their precedence. Completed requests contain
        only compiler warnings, if any. Empty or already-current selections
        can complete with zero updated clones.

        Compiler diagnostic rows have code (COMPILE_ERROR or COMPILE_WARNING),
        file, line, warning (0 or 1), and message. Schema blocker rows have
        code and optional program/name/occurrence. Flattened schema errors
        also have old_generation, candidate_generation, and role
        ("generation" or "blueprint"). Message is optional for schema rows.
        Inheritance blockers add inherit_index (-1 for the whole graph),
        old_inherit_count, and candidate_inherit_count. An identifiable edge
        also adds old_parent, candidate_parent, old_parent_generation,
        candidate_parent_generation, old_mapped, and candidate_mapped.

        Instance rows follow the shared compiler/schema evidence. They have
        code, message, object, role ("clone" or "blueprint"), old_generation,
        and candidate_generation. Object exactly follows object_name() in
        the current COMPAT mode, including its leading slash convention.
        Generation 0 means unavailable metadata on an unsafe/swapped instance.
        SCHEMA_INCOMPATIBLE instance rows link to shared generation evidence.
        Every safely discoverable blocked selected instance is represented, including
        the implicit path-source blueprint; repeated handles with the same
        instance/reason may collapse into one row. Independent runtime and
        named diagnostics are collected after ordinary schema failures.

        HANDLE_DECLARATION_REMOVED and HANDLE_DECLARATION_AMBIGUOUS instance
        rows additionally have kind ("function" or "variable"), program,
        name, occurrence, and dispatch when explicit inherited dispatch needs
        distinguishing. Native closure and Python owners of a declaration
        have separate rows whose messages identify the handle category.
        Private serialized identity keys and handle pointers are not exposed.
        LIVE_CLOSURE, LIVE_COROUTINE, and PYTHON_HANDLE identify unsupported
        live dependencies. Already-current object-source clones need no
        dependency rejection because their program is unchanged.

        Incomplete evidence marks errors[0] with incomplete: 1 and supplies
        incomplete_code and incomplete_message. If a later evidence limit
        follows an earlier primary failure, errors[0] retains its primary
        code/message. A separate limit row identifies that later failure. No omitted-instance count is invented. Byte/work
        limits use REPORT_SIZE_LIMIT and RUNTIME_DEPENDENCY_LIMIT. Complete
        schema/removal evidence survives later diagnostic preparation failure;
        an interrupted schema pass is discarded. Compilation, unavailable
        source identity, or exceptional allocation/callback failure can leave
        only minimal evidence. There is no promise of complete diagnostics
        after an explicit incompleteness marker.

        Other request reasons are:
          UNSUPPORTED_OBJECT, SPECIAL_OBJECT, SOURCE_UNSUPPORTED,
          UNRELATED_TARGET: unsupported role, source, or family membership.
          SOURCE_INVALIDATED, SOURCE_CHANGED: captured source identity,
          program, or loaded blueprint values changed during preparation.
          REPLACEMENT_PENDING: same-family replace_program() conflict.
          SWAP_FAILED: required object/program could not be unswapped.
          COMPILE_FAILED, COMPILE_CALLBACK_FAILED, COMPILE_RESOURCE_FAILED:
          private compilation, its callback, or its resources failed.
          TARGET_LIMIT, SELECTION_SCAN_LIMIT, VARIABLE_STORAGE_LIMIT,
          REQUEST_SIZE_LIMIT: selection or retained preparation bounds.
          REPORT_ALLOCATION_FAILED, PREPARATION_FAILED, RESOURCE_FAILED:
          evidence allocation, preparation, or guarded resource failure.
          SCHEMA_INCOMPATIBLE: see the shared schema blocker details.
          COMPILE_DIAGNOSTIC_LIMIT, SCHEMA_DIAGNOSTIC_LIMIT: bounded evidence
          overflow, distinct from a complete report exactly at the cap.

        Schema blocker codes are MISSING_SCHEMA_METADATA, SCHEMA_DEPTH_LIMIT,
        SCHEMA_WORK_LIMIT, SCHEMA_MEMORY_LIMIT, SCHEMA_DIAGNOSTIC_LIMIT,
        INHERITANCE_CHANGED, MAPPED_INHERITANCE_UNSUPPORTED,
        UNMATCHABLE_VARIABLE, VARIABLE_TYPE_CHANGED, VARIABLE_MODIFIERS_CHANGED,
        FUNCTION_SIGNATURE_CHANGED, FUNCTION_MODIFIERS_CHANGED,
        STRUCT_LAYOUT_CHANGED, MISSING_DEFAULT_METADATA,
        INVALID_DEFAULT_METADATA, UNSUPPORTED_DEFAULT,
        UNAVAILABLE_BLUEPRINT_DEFAULT, DEFAULT_MATERIALIZATION_LIMIT,
        DEFAULT_TYPE_WORK_LIMIT, and DEFAULT_TYPE_MISMATCH. They distinguish
        missing evidence, graph/layout/declaration incompatibility, and
        unavailable or invalid new-variable defaults.

        Compiler evidence is bounded to 128 rows and 65536 text bytes, sharing
        request storage. Each generation/role has at most 128 schema blockers
        plus one overflow row. Runtime instances have no 128-row cap: checked
        count/reservation/fill passes use the shared byte/work budgets.
        See update_blueprint() and blueprint_updates(C) for all limits.

        Each read returns independent nested arrays and mappings, preserving
        sharing within that returned value. Mutation or allocation/evaluation
        failure during copying cannot consume or change the cached report.
        Copying has a fresh bounded allowance and obeys the caller's normal
        array/mapping/evaluation limits; it raises rather than returning a
        partial result. Terminal evidence retains no source/target objects,
        programs, native closures, or Python references. Temporary preparation
        and retirement roots are released before cache eligibility.

        Terminal reports expire at 300 seconds after completion. Reads do not
        renew the time. At most 256 terminal reports are cached; eviction
        removes the oldest completion, breaking ties by the lower ID. This
        cache is separate from pending capacity. Requester destruction removes
        uncommitted requests and failed reports; committed reports remain
        readable by the master until expiry/eviction. Cleanup is automatic.

        The unknown/expired error text is exactly:
          update_blueprint_result(): unknown or expired request id.
        Catch adds its usual leading '*' and the error ends with a newline.
        Other errors, including another owner's ID and copying limits, must
        not be classified as expiry by an operator wrapper.

SEE ALSO
